00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.410
So my name is
Matt McDonald. And

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.480
I live in Stamford, Connecticut
with my wife and daughters. And

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.210
I tell people I'm a statistician
when they ask me what I do.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:14.200
And I've been involved in
data science for quite some time,

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.360
coming up on 20
years, I would say.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.530
I work for Kroll
Bond Rating Agency.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:23.010
So I think we've
rebranded recently,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.365
so we are now known as
just KBRA, I would say. And

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.100
so what does KBRA do? So
we provide credit opinions.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.970
So we're a financial
services company providing credit opinions

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:40.120
in the form of credit
ratings. We've been around since 2010.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.170
So we are a
post-financial crisis credit rating agency.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:46.890
And our

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.610
competitors are companies like
Moody's, S&amp;amp;P, and Fitch.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.940
So these are companies
you may have heard of.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.950
I've been at KBRA since 2015,
so I just surpassed my eight

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.770
year anniversary, so I've
been here for quite some time.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.725
And when I started here, I was

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.150
in charge of model risk
management, which is something

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.720
that if you're in the financial

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.940
services or if you
work for a bank,

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.089
it might be something
that you're familiar with.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.895
I don't know how
widely known it is

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.187
outside of financial services.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.130
But there's a need because
a lot of these companies that

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.670
I've worked for in the past
and that I work for now are

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.890
regulated. There's a need
for controls on the model.

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.640
So we have people who are
dedicated not to building the

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.680
models, but
evaluating the models.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.665
So I started my
career here at KBRA

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.720
in the model risk management
space. I sort of created that

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.480
framework and rolled it out in the company.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.880
And now I'm in charge
of the quantitative modeling group.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.040
So now I'm sort of on the
other side of the fence. And

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.120
I'm in charge of
building the model.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.230
So I run

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.890
a small team of
data scientists,

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.430
econometricians, statisticians,
financial modelers.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.010
And our job is to provide models

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:15.210
to

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.460
credit analysts. So
these are models that

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.365
these people that are used
in the credit rating process.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:24.570
And

00:05:27.106 --> 00:05:29.410
you can group them
into a small--

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.710
you can categorize them.
So a lot of them are like

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.535
statistical models. You can
think about classification models,

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.090
logistic regression
type approach.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.060
And other ones are like
stress testing models.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:43.560
So

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.800
lots of times we'll be looking
at scenarios that could

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.230
happen, but are bad

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.690
and plausible.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.960
And we want to get a sense
of what would happen to

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.190
some financial portfolio or
something along those lines,

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:03.250
right? So we're looking to
see when things get bad,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.480
how bad will things get.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.125
And how does that translate
into our analysis of the credit.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.750
Sometimes we get engaged to

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:13.690
help people

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:15.300
develop

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:20.290
these scenarios, like what
would an adverse scenario for

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:21.490
interest rates look like?

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:23.335
And we get involved in that.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.780
And then there's sort of

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.630
cats and dogs type stuff
around data analysis.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:32.025
So we'll be drawn in and

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:37.330
engaged to help people analyze
sometimes large data sets,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.950
sometimes not large data sets.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:42.640
I'd say that we're not really
in the huge data space.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.620
We're kind of dealing with data
sets that fit into CSV files

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:51.190
that we can look at, right?
So that's kind of what we're

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.320
doing. Those are the types
of models we're doing.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.540
I realized that in

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.030
as I was describing this talk,

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.100
I think I might have said
it's about team building.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.590
And I'm not really going
to talk too much about team

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:06.400
building. I mean,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.350
you might see that I'm reading
some notes here, so I'm trying

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.730
to have some structure
to what I'm doing here.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.155
But I mean,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.015
as far as team building
goes, I always follow the

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.705
guidance of hire
people who are good

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.686
and are talented and that you
like to spend time with. And

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.230
so far, I've been quite
lucky, so I have that largely

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.275
where I am right
now. So that's good.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.560
- What about something you
like to do outside of work too?

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.125
- Oh, yeah. So I
play guitar. And

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.460
actually, I haven't picked
it up a whole lot recently.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:47.360
But once in a while,

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.560
I'll do an open mic type setting
type of a thing. So that's fun.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:54.650
What's that?

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:56.795
- There's lots of
musicians in here.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:01.895
- Oh, nice. We should start
like a jam session someday at

00:08:01.895 --> 00:08:04.535
Posit Data Science
Hangout jam session.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.430
Yeah. Don't rely on
me for singing though.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.310
I do it only out of necessity.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.420
- So Aaron asks what kind
of music do you play?

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.530
- Oh, so I mean, I'm kind
of more of a classic rock guy. But

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:23.790
I don't really have the luxury
of playing the music I like to

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.160
play. It's more of I'm
limited to what I can play.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.130
So three chords
are pretty good.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.475
Singing talent, never
stop Bob Dylan. Nice.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.799
- One other fun
question before we--

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:41.159
they're all fun questions,
but funny question. Last week,

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:46.140
there were a bunch of people
in the chat commenting on their

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:51.103
favorite or worst office
jargon like boil the ocean or--

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:52.395
- Oh, yeah.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.335
- --do you have a
favorite or pet peeve one?

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.250
- No, I mean,

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.020
it's like a bit ephemeral,

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:03.930
like I'll hear it and I'll just
shake my head and say like, oh,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.600
but then I let it go. That's
one of the things. It's

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:11.160
survival skill 101. Let things
go. You can't carry them around.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:15.250
I will say that sometimes you'll hear
someone use a noun as a verb,

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:18.490
so when there's a perfectly
good verb out there to be used.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.970
So someone will say like,
we need to solution this problem

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.480
and that kind of rubs
me the wrong way a lot.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:28.290
There's a word called solve that
would work perfectly fine right there.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:31.940
- So funny. Well,

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.040
I just was getting people going
with that in the chat there. But,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:37.830
Matt,

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:41.490
for some of us that are
not in the financial space or maybe

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.190
familiar with credit
rating agencies,

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.300
would you be able to just
walk us through an example project

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.510
or something that your team
might be building a model for?

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.490
You don't have to get
too into the specifics.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:53.990
- Yeah.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:55.840
Yeah, I mean, I can't--

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.627
I saw a comment in here about
a particular rating that

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:03.880
was issued by KBRA. I
can't really get into that.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:04.660
I was not in--

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.310
I was not directly
involved with that.

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:08.020
One of my models may have been

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:09.760
employed for that.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:12.850
But that's one of the things
that we don't really--

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:13.350
we're

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:17.900
a step away from that ground level.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.130
So we'll build a model
that's available for people to use.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:23.640
And part of our challenge
is to ensure that people

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:27.340
understand its limitations
and understand when it--

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:29.560
and when and when it
should not be used,

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.740
is when it should and
should not be used. I mean,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.600
we've put a lot of thought into

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:40.530
how we get things out into
the world. And we're starting to gel

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:41.790
around

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:45.083
like a playbook that we employ.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:46.270
So

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:53.500
I'll give just a theoretical
example of a model we might

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.290
build. Let's say we wanted to
build a model that would give a

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:58.475
probability of default--

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.320
a classification model.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.940
So that would be like, OK,

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.960
we're going to go
collect data on some

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:09.950
on corporates or maybe banks
or something like that.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:13.430
And we'll think about what
types of things might be

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.130
driving default, right,
and then we'll perform

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:21.395
a statistical analysis,
and we'll build a model.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.260
And maybe that would be like

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.230
the simplest, simplest thing,
a logistic regression model.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.800
So if you tell me a company's--

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.480
some things on
their balance sheet,

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:33.815
something about their cash flow

00:11:35.576 --> 00:11:36.950
and that type of stuff,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:39.710
then maybe we could build
a model that would help you

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:40.970
figure out if that

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:43.205
entity will default.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:46.290
I mean, that's really
half the battle.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.740
And that's a huge battle
because, like I said, we're

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:52.520
regulated, and we have
internal controls who are

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:54.840
evaluating our work.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.120
So we need to really
make sure that all

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.340
of our work is
repeatable and transparent and inspectable,

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:07.280
and we can explain it. And
it's well-documented. And then

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.620
we have the expectation
that there's evidence-based.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:13.535
So we perform back testing
and we present that.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:15.920
But the

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:18.710
next problem--

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:20.270
and this is when
I say playbook,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:23.720
I'm talking about
is how do we make--

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.055
once that model has

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.820
been built, how do we allow
that model to be used by

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.230
people, by real people

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:32.005
who are doing a job?

00:12:33.945 --> 00:12:35.450
And we are--

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.070
I mean shameless plug for Posit,
so we use the Posit, team's platform.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:44.660
So that allows us to do
our work in a common environment.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.850
And we have

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.620
controls in place that help
us make things repeatable.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.770
We're using Git,
we're using RN.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:13:00.260
If we're building an R
then we're making sure that our

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:03.800
environments are sort of repeat--

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:07.505
we can build them
up. And then we are--

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.920
I mean, we're not 100% there,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.270
but we've been experimenting
with things like Vetiver and

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:17.885
plumber for deploying
models via API that

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:20.135
are then usable by people.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.585
One of the things that we

00:13:24.545 --> 00:13:29.260
have been really working on and
really focusing on is like establishing

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.760
ourselves. I mean,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:34.565
the conundrum that we have
is that we're a small team,

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:37.190
and we kind of sit between

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:43.472
a technology organization,
we're building software and

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:50.600
credit analysts who are doing
the work of credit analysis.

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:56.090
So like the tool kits between
those two folks and between us are

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:57.860
radically different.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.520
I would say the analysts
are largely using Microsoft Excel

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.450
for a lot of their work, the

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:11.130
technology folks are
using Python mostly for--

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.405
like in a software
development context. And

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:16.680
I'm an advocate of using the

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:21.890
right tool for the job so we're
using Python and R in more of a

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.185
scripting context to

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.550
do our analysis and
build our model.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:28.050
So

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:32.750
being able to talk to
all of those different parties has

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:35.070
been something that we've
been really working on.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.265
And we've had a
lot of success with

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:42.050
the tools that we've chosen in making--

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.225
breaking down some of the
barriers that we face.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:45.035
-

00:14:45.035 --> 00:14:48.800
Can you expand upon that a bit
maybe for some of us who are having--

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:50.720
who are kind of in the
middle of that right now?

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:54.065
How do you break down
some of those barriers between teams?

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.830
- Yeah, I mean, it's like--

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:00.530
it's a balancing act,
I would say, between

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.340
reaching out to people and

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:07.620
operating on their turf.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:09.583
We're talking their language

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:15.000
but also working very hard
to retain some sort of

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:16.820
line

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.710
of ownership of things. So

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.660
I would say like when
we do deploy an API,

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:28.605
we've had some success and
done some experimentation with

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.500
providing people
with some code,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:35.790
PBA code in their Excel
spreadsheet to call our API so

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:36.960
that they don't need to learn--

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:40.170
really learn a new
skill to use our work.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:43.830
And going the other way,
some of our projects,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.930
some of our models
are not really that

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.225
regression-based model where

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:56.010
all I have is a set of data
and I'm going to create a

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:58.620
prediction. Some of them are
really more software type

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:00.580
models. They're
more complicated.

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.460
These are like
simulation models,

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:05.490
Monte Carlo simulations
where we're going to--

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.520
that's a pretty standard
and credit risk modeling,

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.335
like a pretty
standard approach.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:13.990
And so you need to build
software to do that.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:14.895
And when we--

00:16:15.885 --> 00:16:16.875
I want to--

00:16:16.875 --> 00:16:20.850
I don't want to build,
own, and maintain software.

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:25.050
That's sort of a job unto
itself. So we do engage with

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.493
technology, and that's
like when we go there,

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:32.970
we've sort of decided that they
really don't speak R so much so

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:37.500
we will have to do more
Python stuff. And switch our mindset

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:41.970
of how we're using Python
from that idea of performing some

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:46.780
analysis, writing a script
that does a thing into writing

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:48.617
Python code that

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:53.095
is integrated in like a
software development type of a project.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:54.370
So

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:02.580
I'd say that we've been had
a lot of success with learning

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:06.569
the tools that are employed
by technology and trying to figure

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:08.609
out how we apply
those to our work. So

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:11.649
everybody on the team

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:15.369
has been learning Git,
we really are getting--

00:17:15.369 --> 00:17:16.569
we're very good at Git.

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:21.069
And we find that Git is a super
useful tool not just for--

00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:24.579
like I said earlier, we
need all of our work to be

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.904
repeatable because we could be

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:31.930
engaged by a whole bunch of
people who are asking us to

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:33.025
explain what we've done.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.860
So Git is super
helpful for that.

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:40.180
All of our code is transparent,
inspectable. People can

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:42.550
see all the changes that
have happened over time.

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:46.960
But also we're using some
of the project management

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.920
tools that they have.
So things like Jira

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:55.160
and Wiki-based
documentation, things are--

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.130
we've had a lot
of success with.

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:57.760
- Thank you.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.150
I see Julia you had a follow
up question here on the

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:03.885
repeatable work. Do
you want to jump in?

00:18:03.885 --> 00:18:07.390
JULIA: Sure, thanks. This
is really interesting.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:10.970
So you were just touching on
how your work needs to be like

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.370
replicable and transparent.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:17.120
Does that extend also to your
models? Do your models need to

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:23.150
be explainable or is it OK
to use black box models as long

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:27.650
as the process to derive them
has been transparent in your industry?

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.720
- Yeah, no, I think that

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.400
there's no regulatory
need or sort of like

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:37.200
nothing dictating what kind of

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:42.630
algorithms we can use to build
our models. But I would say we

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.877
definitely stay on
the side of simpler,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:48.080
maybe

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.350
slightly less
performative models

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:53.670
that are explainable.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:57.540
Because it's more of like not only
do I have to explain it to

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:01.740
the credit analysts, but they
probably need to explain to

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:06.200
a lot of other people how they
arrived at their decision.

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.760
So if we have just an
absolute black box,

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:14.930
that doesn't really work for us.
Although we have used some of those

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:20.230
approaches to help us benchmark
our models. So when you

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:23.560
have like a matrix of

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.060
model performance and

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:28.915
interpretability, I
would say, would be

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.570
kind of a graph I've
seen in the past,

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.710
and we're kind of on
the interpretable end of the spectrum.

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:35.910
But

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.070
lots of times we'll
ask the question like,

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:43.140
what are we leaving on the
table as far as model performance

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:45.590
when we make that decision?

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:50.050
So we'll employ like a more black
box type approach to say like, look,

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:53.260
if we didn't care if we
could explain this to anybody ever,

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:54.700
what could we achieve?

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:59.095
And we use that in our
decision making process.

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:03.000
- Thank you. Andy, I see
you had a question a bit earlier.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:03.845
Do you to jump in here? ANDY NUNEZ

00:20:03.845 --> 00:20:06.830
BREWSTER: Sure. Thanks. Hi.
I'm Andy Nunez

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:10.670
Brewster. And my question was about the

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.940
looking ahead if you're using--

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:15.560
you did answer the
question actually--

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.470
you're using some simulation
model versus just data driven

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:23.510
exploration. And I was curious if
you're doing that in R or

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.730
what's the stack on that
side of the house. Thanks.

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:26.950
- Yeah.

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:30.425
Yeah, I would say like the--

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:31.550
we--

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:35.510
I mean, we use both Python
and R for our more backward

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:39.215
looking statistical data
driven models and then

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.730
those models that are
more simulation-based or

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:48.140
Monte Carlo type things
just simply because we are

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:54.390
eager to tap into some of
the good stuff that our technology

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:55.530
team has been building.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:57.870
We're primarily--

00:20:57.870 --> 00:20:59.490
if we're going to
be writing software,

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:04.950
aside from internal R
packages that are really--

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:06.960
like, my team is the
audience for those.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:10.980
We're going to build a package that's
going to be help us do something.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:13.230
We'll use R for that.

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:16.200
But if we're going to be
building software that's going to be deployed

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:22.240
across the company, then we'll
be looking to Python mostly.

00:21:27.978 --> 00:21:31.830
We've been experimenting
with the very simple and

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:32.961
straightforward--

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:37.830
I think I've heard the term
microservice but I probably am misusing it,

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:40.680
like the idea of like I have
some data and I want to get a

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:41.955
prediction from that data.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:43.980
We've been working with--

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:45.210
like I said--

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.210
Plumber and Vetiver

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.940
or those types of things
where we can deploy that.

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:52.526
And those get deployed to our

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:55.035
Posit Connect server.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:21:59.800
The software stuff

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:04.510
happens mostly as a
collaborative effort between us

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:05.680
and our technology

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:09.220
folks in Python.

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:11.590
But another thing that we've
had a lot of success with

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:16.760
is when we're doing more
of an analysis or helping somebody

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:18.930
maybe--

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.220
we've actually written a few

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:25.525
research pieces, mostly
internal research pieces in--

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:28.310
we've been using
Quarto quite a bit.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:33.760
So Quarto is a very, very
useful tool to create documents

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:36.115
that are sort of very closely

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:38.220
interwoven

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:42.004
with the underlying data, and

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:45.850
that's been a
super useful tool.

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:49.900
And that kind of gets us out
of the business of emailing

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:54.670
spreadsheets and Word documents
and that type of stuff.

00:22:55.540 --> 00:22:58.285
I much, much, much
prefer the pattern of

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:02.200
publishing a document, we
publish it to our Connect

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.720
server, and sharing
a link with somebody.

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:07.850
And then if I need to
update it, I can update it there.

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:11.200
And there's even some really
nice features that we've seen

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.320
about. Like you can
have the same code,

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:16.010
the same Quarto document

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:20.290
generating the web page
that someone's looking at,

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:23.305
and downloading it as a PDF. And the PDFs

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.875
look really nice so we've
had some really good success there.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:31.860
- Awesome. I see there
was a question about

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:34.750
Git a bit earlier
here. Let me ask it.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:38.150
I see there's a star there.
What resources, if any,

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:39.625
did you use to help your team

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.610
get on board with using Git?

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:45.580
Were you all pretty familiar
with it already or did anyone

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.920
on the team start learning
from the ground up?

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:52.710
- I would say we've been
learning from the ground up. And

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:56.500
I was thinking about
this recently that

00:23:57.730 --> 00:23:59.080
I must have taken

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:01.960
10 different times--

00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:04.720
like I said, all right, I'm
going to learn Git. And I go

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:09.520
take some sort of online
course and sort of learn the

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:13.630
fundamentals, and it never
stuck. I just was like,

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:15.310
I don't really
get this too much.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.320
And it wasn't until

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:20.230
actually we were having
help from the folks in our

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:24.990
technology team on how to set
up our repos, what kind of

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:28.270
patters

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:31.610
do we use when we collaborate?

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:35.930
I just today was told
we were using a trunk-based approach

00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:37.470
for when we do our

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:41.070
branches and merges so that
was kind of news to me.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:46.510
But I would say none of it really
stuck until I was put in a position--

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:51.935
or I put myself in a position where
I had to use it. And then once I--

00:24:51.935 --> 00:24:56.600
I mean, once we started
collaborating across the team and I--

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:58.640
that's when I really
saw the power of it.

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:00.830
Because I'm from a place--

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:02.210
before Git, I mean,

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:04.130
back in my day,

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:08.195
it was like you'd have
some code and then you'd--

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:10.190
if you needed to change it,

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:12.540
you just were praying
that it didn't break.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:14.780
And if it broke, you
kind of were sitting there thinking,

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:19.790
oh boy, I hope I remember
what I did so I can undo it.

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:22.880
And it's really liberating
to not have that kind of--

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:26.990
be carrying around that mental
luggage all the time about what

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:31.730
have I changed over the last
two hours and could I recover

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:33.860
what I had before. So, I mean,

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:35.600
I'm a huge Git
advocate right now.

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:36.100
Like

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:40.160
there's a really
good paper out there,

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:43.460
good enough practices
for scientific computing.

00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:46.880
I think Jenny Bryant who's
a Posit employee is a co-author

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:48.632
of that thing. And
that's a really good--

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:51.680
she also has a

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:55.440
Git for R, I think,

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.210
or something like that
or some book like that.

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:57.410
- Yeah,

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:00.530
there's a lot of love for that
resource in the chat right now too,

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:02.330
Happy Git
with R.

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:04.860
- Happy Git with
R, yeah, that's the one.

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:08.520
And that's like even nowadays
if I'm doing something and I'm

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:11.520
not collaborating with
anyone and I'm in our studio,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.110
I'm starting up a
new project, I mean,

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:16.590
I'm clicking that Git repo
button every time because that's like--

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:17.850
that's my safety net.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:21.060
So I'd say I don't
have, really--

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:21.460
I mean,

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.660
we were lucky we had people
in our organization that helped us

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:26.550
get over the hump, but

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:31.440
the best way to get
familiar with it is use it.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.915
So it's kind of a catch 22, I think,

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:35.240
there.

