I get a little bit nervous
about with Shiny is like it's

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:16.440
such an amazing tool.

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:19.650
I would say that we've kind of
limited our use of that to more

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:22.080
of the model development piece.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:26.220
So when we're building a model
and we want to communicate to

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:30.850
the users or the model
owners or the subject matter experts,

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:34.110
the people who know the
stuff, like, how this model works.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:36.340
If I put in this input,
what kind of output--

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:37.875
what kind of
sensitivities am I seeing.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.610
Shiny has been a really
great tool for that,

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:48.870
my concern about Shiny is if
I build something that is super awesome--

00:28:48.870 --> 00:28:51.540
which is really
accessible with Shiny.

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:55.350
You can build really amazing
things. My concern would be that

00:28:56.982 --> 00:29:01.420
I don't view myself as
like somebody who writes production code.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:05.680
So if it were to become part
of a core part of people's

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:07.485
day to day lives,

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:11.190
I'd get nervous about something
along the lines of getting a

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:14.130
call at 11:00 PM that the
Shiny app is down and,

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:15.330
can you fix it, Matt?

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.170
And I'm the only guy
who knows how to fix it.

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:21.720
So it's like a bit of a
double edged sword so we do try to

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:27.750
really leverage our technology teams
when it comes to building core

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.680
functionality, software
functionality that people need.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:35.910
So it's like with great
power comes great responsibility type

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:37.620
of a message I'm saying there.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:39.930
But we've had great success
with Tiny. I think it's--

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:40.320
Shiny--

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:44.350
it's a really great tool
but mostly in the prototyping and

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:48.220
early result stage,
I would say. Yeah.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:49.010
- Do you hand-- MICHAEL

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:50.980
THOMAS: I hear that.
No, thank you.

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:51.715
- I'm sorry.

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:55.315
Do you hand it over to
that team as an APIs?

00:29:55.315 --> 00:29:57.310
Is that the workflow you
were talking about earlier?

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:01.100
- So, yeah, we would try to--

00:30:01.100 --> 00:30:03.980
if that model we
would either try to--

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:06.530
I mean, we haven't really fully

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:10.670
executed on this vision but we
would be looking to deploy the

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:12.635
model as an API
and then allow--

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:18.740
reduce the bar of accessibility to
people there so that they could--

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:20.720
if they wanted to
perform some analysis,

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:23.030
they could kind of
do it themselves.

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:27.950
Or the other path would be
like try to work with technology to

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.620
build some sort of software that would be

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:32.050
in our

00:30:32.930 --> 00:30:34.940
stable of software internally,

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:38.755
built software that people are
using to get things done. Yeah.

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:40.550
- Thank you.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.685
Allan, I see you had a
question. You want to jump in here?

00:30:44.685 --> 00:30:46.050
ALLAN: Yeah. Hi.

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:51.090
Matt, this is a super
naive question about the industry.

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:55.650
And I'm curious about how the work
that you do translates over to what

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.190
the analysts are
taking to customers.

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:01.935
And I'm assuming that
there's something that differentiates like

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:06.540
that's your value proposition for,
like our analysis is better

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.880
because you've got
competitors in the industry.

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:13.080
And so I wonder if you
can just give some context to, what

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:19.170
makes a report successful to
you and to your customers?

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:20.280
And how do you--

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:21.950
how

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:25.740
does that factor into how do
you plan an analysis or how do

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:26.580
you plan a model?

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:31.530
You can come at any
way that is useful. Just wondering,

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:33.600
how does this data fit into
the big picture of what your

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.415
customers are looking for?

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:36.440
- Sure.

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:38.492
I mean, that's a good question.

00:31:39.336 --> 00:31:39.836
So

00:31:40.540 --> 00:31:44.860
the way our business works is we
have a lot of different ratings teams,

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:48.700
so all of those teams are sort
of experts in their field.

00:31:49.360 --> 00:31:51.460
There's a whole bunch of
different types of things that

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:54.930
we're rating. So we
rate corporates, we rate banks.

00:31:55.630 --> 00:31:58.330
And then there's this whole
other side of the fence where

00:31:58.330 --> 00:32:01.320
it's like people
issue debt that are--

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:02.380
that's backed by

00:32:03.055 --> 00:32:05.300
some sort of cash
generating assets.

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:07.660
So that's called
structured finance.

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:12.970
So like residential mortgage backed
securities is a thing out there,

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.150
commercial mortgage
backed securities.

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:20.560
So each one of those things
has its team of subject matter

00:32:20.560 --> 00:32:23.450
experts that are
in charge of doing

00:32:24.130 --> 00:32:24.970
the credit analysis.

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:28.120
They know all about
what drives the credit,

00:32:28.120 --> 00:32:31.555
what could make the credit not
pay. They know all that stuff.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:32.700
So

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:37.720
each one of those groups have
one or more, I think we call

00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:38.740
them methodologies.

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:42.730
So these are public statements
to the world that describe

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:44.770
everything that goes into--

00:32:44.770 --> 00:32:50.320
it's kind of like the playbook. It's
not as sort of step one, two, three,

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:54.130
it's not like a recipe book
but if you know the industry,

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:56.820
you could pick that thing up and
you would have a sense of what is--

00:32:57.320 --> 00:33:01.480
how do we do it? What
are we thinking about when we--

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:02.560
and I say we--

00:33:03.460 --> 00:33:04.840
our company, KBRA,

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:07.230
does a credit rating.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:07.730
So

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:12.220
the models we build are
generally like a component of

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:13.900
that. So we will build--

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:17.150
there's qualitative,
quantitative aspects of

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:20.060
the process, and our

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:25.550
work flows into the quantitative
piece of that. But it's

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:28.030
all part of a broader thing.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:32.950
So I would say that KBRA
differentiates itself with like we have-- like

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:33.850
I said--

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:38.230
really strong credit rating
experts who do the work.

00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:42.280
And they do quite extensive
write ups on everything, every

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:45.130
consideration they have when
they're creating the ratings. So

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:48.430
hopefully that

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:51.940
helps illustrate a little
bit of what's going on there.

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:53.950
ALLAN: Yeah, yeah,
it does. Thank you. MATT

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:54.625
MCDONALD: Yeah.

