I guess I'm thinking too like
about the communication part of it. How

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:51.310
do you work with them to help
people see the value in some of

00:42:51.310 --> 00:42:54.310
the models or do they
see that initially?

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:55.950
- Yeah,

00:42:56.650 --> 00:42:59.320
well, I mean, I would love to

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:03.940
solve all of the problems
I currently have and put myself

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:05.320
in a position to start

00:43:06.190 --> 00:43:09.220
generating more internal
business for myself by

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:12.862
convincing people to trust
their models more and

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:17.940
use

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:20.170
them more and more and more.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:23.950
But we kind of are dealing
with the problem in front of us

00:43:23.950 --> 00:43:26.260
right now, I would say, yeah.

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:29.560
- Another question I saw just
pop into the chat, Phil asked,

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:30.670
in your organization,

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:34.810
do you have to justify or
submit for approval to an IT or

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:35.785
regulatory group?

00:43:38.350 --> 00:43:40.220
- I mean, yeah.

00:43:40.900 --> 00:43:43.020
I mean, we have a--

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:45.645
it's a whole thing.

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:47.300
So we need to

00:43:48.100 --> 00:43:52.620
describe the project we're making and
then that goes where we're proposing.

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:57.830
And then that goes into a
prioritization decision making

00:43:57.830 --> 00:43:58.745
type of a thing.

00:44:01.250 --> 00:44:05.960
I mean, it's not quite as
formalized as other places I've

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:07.130
seen. So it's not like

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:09.330
I'm filling out like Form

00:44:10.370 --> 00:44:10.870
T95

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:13.880
or anything like
that. But there is--

00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:16.200
I mean, we, obviously--

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:17.996
I mean, we're a
business, right, so we

00:44:19.940 --> 00:44:22.200
need to work on the things
that are most important

00:44:22.880 --> 00:44:26.120
to us. Yeah, so definitely. But

00:44:28.124 --> 00:44:29.540
I'm sort of lucky that

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:33.605
I've been here for quite
some time. A lot of the--

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:37.955
I'd say that early
on while I was here,

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:41.360
a lot of what I was doing was

00:44:42.080 --> 00:44:44.570
work to gain people's trust,

00:44:44.570 --> 00:44:46.550
and that's a super
important thing.

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:48.760
And

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:53.060
not to pat myself on the back
too hard but I did it right.

00:44:53.870 --> 00:44:57.095
I have worked hard to make sure
that people can trust me. So like

00:44:58.280 --> 00:44:58.970
this most--

00:44:58.970 --> 00:45:01.910
we just recently kicked off a
new project where it's more of

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:06.530
that software development type of
a lane that we're getting into.

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:09.920
And that one for a
long time I was really

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:15.560
trying to figure out how do I
do this job. I don't want to be--

00:45:15.560 --> 00:45:17.160
I'm not a software developer,

00:45:17.160 --> 00:45:19.400
but I kind of play
one on TV sometimes.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:22.340
How do I do this job where

00:45:23.240 --> 00:45:26.640
I can keep a low profile
and do things autonomously?

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:29.210
And I concluded that

00:45:30.330 --> 00:45:33.240
there are certain things that
you do need to get in the

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:37.680
queue, get in that
prioritization sort of game,

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:42.330
and that's the best--

00:45:42.330 --> 00:45:43.830
that's going to be
the best outcome.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:47.430
Because you don't really
want to be creating in an

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:49.220
organization like a shadow IT

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:52.000
or organization or
anything like that.

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:52.500
I think

00:45:53.130 --> 00:45:57.720
that it's great for getting things
done but I think down the line,

00:45:57.720 --> 00:46:00.810
we pay a lot of people who
really know a lot about

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:02.940
technology to do that job.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:05.955
I don't want to have
another one of those

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:09.295
run by a handful
of people I have

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:14.685
that become really important. So
I'd rather be on their turf.

00:46:15.430 --> 00:46:15.515
-

00:46:15.515 --> 00:46:19.350
Are there questions that you'd like
to ask the group here or

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:24.270
things that you'd love to
maybe open up to more of a

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:26.640
discussion on? I'm trying to

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:30.900
think back to something we
were talking about at the conference

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:32.635
as well and we were at.

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:35.440
- I hadn't really
contemplated that.

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:37.615
I thought you guys were
going to ask me questions.

00:46:37.615 --> 00:46:38.560
- Right, no, I can

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:40.390
keep going on this,

00:46:40.390 --> 00:46:43.495
I just wanted to make sure
that if there are certain

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:47.400
problems that you're facing
that you're just curious to

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:50.220
open up to ask everybody here,

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:52.770
that we give the time
for that as well.

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:53.270
- Yeah.

00:46:55.230 --> 00:46:57.330
If somebody has any good ideas

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:02.790
after hearing what we're dealing with.
I mean, a lot of the

00:47:05.940 --> 00:47:06.870
difficulties

00:47:07.670 --> 00:47:10.865
we've encountered being
in this role between

00:47:11.960 --> 00:47:15.035
the credit rating analysts
who are doing their job,

00:47:15.810 --> 00:47:16.740
they want to rate--

00:47:16.740 --> 00:47:20.240
they want to do the credit
analysis and technology who's

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:26.000
also doing their job is that
it really opened us up to

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:29.900
being just people not really
understanding what our role is.

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:34.310
So lots of times we
really do get confused with technology.

00:47:34.310 --> 00:47:36.140
Like we'll get a call from--

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:38.300
I'll get a call from
senior leadership,

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:40.700
people who are very senior
in the company, and say like,

00:47:40.700 --> 00:47:42.800
I need this thing
that does X, Y, and Z.

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:45.140
And they're not
describing a model,

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:47.600
they're not describing
like really anything

00:47:48.860 --> 00:47:50.975
I do or I'm really good at.

00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:52.400
So

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:55.070
that sort of establishing--

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:59.420
like trying to continually
hammer home like what--

00:47:59.420 --> 00:48:01.100
describe to people
what is it that--

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:02.570
what would you say you do here.

00:48:03.716 --> 00:48:04.640
That's something that

00:48:06.472 --> 00:48:08.400
I've sort of encountered a lot,

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:11.130
that clarity and making
sure that people understand that.

00:48:11.130 --> 00:48:12.780
And then just that idea of

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:14.670
trying to really--

00:48:15.330 --> 00:48:20.340
because we are a smaller
team and we do have limited

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:23.745
resources, just establishing
those lines of ownership

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:26.775
and helping people without

00:48:29.335 --> 00:48:33.740
doing their job is kind of
a thing that can be difficult too.

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:36.070
So those are things that
kind of need to be managed,

00:48:36.070 --> 00:48:37.900
I would say. And
that's what a lot of--

00:48:38.770 --> 00:48:41.680
I wouldn't say I spent a lot of
time on it but I've spent a lot

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:43.390
of time thinking
about it in the past.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:46.850
A lot of the things
we've done around

00:48:48.340 --> 00:48:52.900
using the right tools to
get our work repeatable and this

00:48:52.900 --> 00:48:56.830
type of thing and being able
to engage with technology on their

00:48:56.830 --> 00:49:01.110
turf in certain instances
has really been beneficial.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:05.890
- I see a lot
of great questions jumping into--

00:49:05.890 --> 00:49:07.390
or coming into the chat here.

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:11.790
Laura, do you want to
ask your question here?

00:49:11.790 --> 00:49:12.490
LAURA: Sure.

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:14.485
I'm going to stay off camera
because I'm a little under the weather.

00:49:15.790 --> 00:49:17.845
So my question is,
have there ever been

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:21.910
situations where a rating
against maybe a portfolio or a

00:49:21.910 --> 00:49:23.365
company has come out

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:28.610
against what the gut check
is? I'm always interested in those

00:49:28.610 --> 00:49:32.000
situations where an analysis
goes against the initial

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:35.040
hypothesis and how
organizations handle that.

00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:37.595
So I wonder if you have
some situations and how you've navigated those.

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:41.340
- Yeah. I mean,
I would say that we--

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:41.640
I mean,

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:44.430
none of our ratings are
like the model spits out the

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:47.290
ratings, and that's what we
go with. So it's always--

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:50.850
there's always a level of
human oversight and human

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:52.820
intervention
there, I would say.

00:49:52.820 --> 00:49:54.060
I mean, obviously,

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:57.510
with any model you're going
to have some outcome that somebody

00:49:57.510 --> 00:49:59.865
who really knows the
domain is going to say,

00:50:00.750 --> 00:50:02.440
no, I don't think
that's the deal.

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:04.080
I don't think that's
what it is. But

00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:06.130
our methodologies

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:09.940
really accommodate
for that. These are--

00:50:09.940 --> 00:50:14.460
there's always a
qualitative and quantitative component.

00:50:14.460 --> 00:50:17.640
And, ultimately, the
way the process works,

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:21.930
there's a rating committee and the
decision is made not by my

00:50:21.930 --> 00:50:24.405
model. My model is an
input into that process.

00:50:26.830 --> 00:50:29.022
Part of the reason why we--

00:50:29.886 --> 00:50:33.030
earlier I said we rely--

00:50:33.030 --> 00:50:38.260
given the choice between a
model whose accuracy ratio is

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:40.270
1% greater than
this other model,

00:50:40.270 --> 00:50:44.740
that I can explain to somebody
how this model got this answer.

00:50:44.740 --> 00:50:48.040
I'm pretty much always going
to go with the explainability part.

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:50.770
And your question
is kind of like--

00:50:50.770 --> 00:50:55.270
is part of the reason for that.
Is like if I get a question,

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:57.040
why did the model
give me this answer?

00:50:57.790 --> 00:51:02.020
I'm very happy to explain to
them, and then that could drive

00:51:02.020 --> 00:51:05.410
a conversation about the
limitations of the model.

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:09.550
Maybe that would be an
indication that maybe it's time--

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:10.340
maybe this model

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:15.040
has exceeded its utility
and we need to build a--

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:17.865
re-estimate it, build a
new one that has other

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:21.310
risk drivers in it.
Those types of things.

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:23.220
So it definitely happens.

00:51:23.940 --> 00:51:26.400
We're never really put in
a position where something goes

00:51:26.400 --> 00:51:30.555
out the door that sort of came
out of model and nobody looked at.

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:31.650
- RM,

00:51:31.650 --> 00:51:34.650
I see you have a question in the chat
