And so far it's worked.

00:54:06.870 --> 00:54:08.370
We really haven't had this--

00:54:08.370 --> 00:54:10.190
that sort of like a

00:54:10.800 --> 00:54:12.390
moment of like, oh,

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:15.780
do I do this really
important thing or that really important

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:20.160
thing? We've been able to use
that to help us not only like

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:22.060
not forget to do things--

00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:22.815
which is a huge

00:54:23.910 --> 00:54:24.390
benefit--

00:54:24.390 --> 00:54:26.330
but manage our workload.

00:54:27.030 --> 00:54:33.090
But it also gets back to that
idea of building trust. So

00:54:33.090 --> 00:54:34.635
I've been lucky that

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:39.720
I've had the opportunity to

00:54:44.149 --> 00:54:45.834
do things that are--

00:54:47.130 --> 00:54:51.600
had that overlap between stuff
that my company needs to get

00:54:51.600 --> 00:54:54.420
done and stuff that I'm
interested in doing.

00:54:54.420 --> 00:54:58.545
So that's been really good. And
it's been nice to be able to

00:55:00.330 --> 00:55:02.400
call the shots along the way.

00:55:03.636 --> 00:55:05.010
ALLAN: Nice. Thanks.

00:55:06.610 --> 00:55:09.070
- Geoffrey just put a
question to the chat.

00:55:09.070 --> 00:55:10.910
How does your team
work together?

00:55:10.910 --> 00:55:13.630
Do you have a data engineer
that pulls data, passes

00:55:14.380 --> 00:55:15.420
the data scientist,

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:18.310
or does that--

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:21.130
the person that creates the model
do it all or something else?

00:55:22.030 --> 00:55:26.230
- Yeah, I mean, it really is
on a project by project basis,

00:55:26.230 --> 00:55:29.515
that sort of the genesis of
what that data set looks like.

00:55:31.630 --> 00:55:35.230
Sometimes the team who's responsible
for the rating have some--

00:55:35.230 --> 00:55:39.220
have access to really great data
sets that we can use to

00:55:39.220 --> 00:55:39.890
build our models.

00:55:40.570 --> 00:55:41.695
Sometimes it's about

00:55:43.766 --> 00:55:45.930
pulling it together from

00:55:47.170 --> 00:55:49.650
publicly available
sources like the

00:55:50.700 --> 00:55:53.730
Federal Reserve economic database
or other types of

00:55:53.730 --> 00:55:57.090
things, and pulling that
together. I will say that--

00:55:57.090 --> 00:56:01.530
I did neglect to say we do have
embedded on our team like a

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:05.860
folks who are that
MLOps type role.

00:56:05.860 --> 00:56:11.440
So they're more technologists who
are helping us maintain our platform.

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:15.370
And also there are crackerjack
coders who could sort step in

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:18.640
and give us some feedback
on how to do things,

00:56:18.640 --> 00:56:22.810
how to make Git work better and
also how to engage with some of

00:56:22.810 --> 00:56:24.830
the internal resources
and technology.

00:56:24.830 --> 00:56:27.010
So we do have some folks on
the team who are more of that

00:56:27.010 --> 00:56:30.400
technology role who are
helping us get things done.

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:34.510
And sometimes they'll be
useful in that sort of data--

00:56:34.510 --> 00:56:36.970
more the upfront
data acquisition.

00:56:38.140 --> 00:56:43.000
- I know we talked a little
bit about trust or came up a few

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:44.620
times, and I was just wondering

00:56:45.280 --> 00:56:49.060
what are some of those ways
you were able to build trust with

00:56:49.060 --> 00:56:50.380
the stakeholders.

00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:50.880
- Yeah.

00:56:51.490 --> 00:56:53.380
I'd say like early on--

00:56:53.380 --> 00:56:53.920
and it was--

